    "scenario": "An 8 month-old boy admitted with dehydration has the following initial labs: serum Na 128, urine Na 20, serum Cr 2.0 and urine Cr 4.0. Following two 20 mL/kg normal saline boluses the repeat labs indicate a serum Na 130, a serum Cr of 1.5 and unchanged urine values. These values indicate which of the following?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "The presence of intrinsic renal dysfunction",
      "B": "The values are unreliable in the presence of dehydration",
      "C": "The decreases are due to dilution by the boluses",
      "D": "Hypovolemia resulting from dehydration",
      "E": "The need for diuretics to reduce the free water excess"
    },
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctAnswerText": "The presence of intrinsic renal dysfunction",
    "explanation": "FeNa = (UNa × PCr)/(PNa × UCr) × 100 = (20 × 2.0)/(128 × 4.0) × 100 ≈ 7.8%. After boluses it is (20 × 1.5)/(130 × 4) × 100 ≈ 5.8%. A FeNa above 2–3% indicates intrinsic renal dysfunction (ATN) rather than prerenal azotemia.",
